#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148392} :activity/id #uuid "0d7d228b-78f0-507d-898b-cc2078977de4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048131 :topic/id #uuid "81e52842-e868-5110-93ce-65b6024b5770" :topic/math-academy-id 2533} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148392 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148771 :tutorial/content "There are three types of equilibrium solutions for an *autonomous* differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = f(y),\n$$\n\nwhere the right-hand side doesn't depend explicitly on $x.$\nWe can classify each using the differential equation's phase line.\n\nLet's see each type, and classify the equilibrium solutions for the differential equation whose phase line is shown below.\n\n![](images/c6/c6fc21e9c83b98e055751ebd4062a3019687a885e2561191ec2d0bfd9de36a85.png)\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a **sink** if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\nFor the phase line above, $y=2$ is a sink.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a **source** if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution. \nFor the phase line above, $y=0$ is a source.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a **node** if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\nFor the phase line above, $y=-2$ is a node since the arrows above and below it both point downward." :tutorial/id #uuid "2f2f0bc9-a043-5c8e-92aa-88135e4033a9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5572 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2e343aa9-f699-5b3e-a382-effdfef6dd15" :step/math-academy-id 594276 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148393}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148393 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147494 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147389 :question/id #uuid "bb252f4f-ef82-52bc-a485-f88f0de96173" :question/math-academy-id "e-8650"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049741}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395535}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines"} :step/id #uuid "29698ec0-1dda-5f69-bb67-f92175c1a371" :step/math-academy-id 594277 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148394}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148394 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147495 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147387 :question/id #uuid "7aa0a0a8-05d4-532c-a830-f1df43514fdf" :question/math-academy-id "e-8538"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049905}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375611}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "f7615658-7aa8-5de3-95ff-2d74b5889e16" :step/math-academy-id 594280 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148395}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148395 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147496 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147385 :question/id #uuid "eed50b24-e97c-51c9-b625-3327783b6374" :question/math-academy-id "e-8536"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b114a373-697f-5005-bb1b-46202547a905" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374986}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying the Equilibrium Points of a Given Differential Equation"} :step/id #uuid "060c8c47-3b5f-5a36-b80e-757102d6f065" :step/math-academy-id 594283 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148396}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148396 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147497 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147386 :question/id #uuid "dba1deed-4311-54fe-a690-8d89d4cb9592" :question/math-academy-id "e-8537"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58b04cc-da5e-5c51-a583-0663d783c697" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049722}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414153}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Slope Fields Corresponding to Some Equilibrium Point Classifications"} :step/id #uuid "5b0fcd37-0446-59cb-b89e-24c477e9a1cc" :step/math-academy-id 594286}] :activity/title "Classifying Equilibrium Solutions of First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147712}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414157} :activity/id #uuid "8f67b1d8-cc12-5be2-bedd-7d8ea39d1438" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048131 :topic/id #uuid "81e52842-e868-5110-93ce-65b6024b5770" :topic/math-academy-id 2533} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414154 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28ce5dc1-3ad1-5783-b204-8bdf6241851e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312284} {:answer/id #uuid "2d61944a-2c9c-57a5-889e-9eebe6233da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312332} {:answer/id #uuid "55b50611-292b-5b43-be49-4298a1c8b4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312663} {:answer/id #uuid "710c17b1-dce6-52ad-ab8a-e1595333c2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312864} {:answer/id #uuid "8c772e93-19a4-564e-a239-51cd6da30785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313099}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312284} :answer-field/id #uuid "7167d3ef-020b-5c26-98b0-e6af7ebfa802" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a963a554-e172-5944-b345-2fca4048d858" :question/math-academy-id "q-339709" :question/problem "![](images/78/78c4fafeaa4015c98d2a1817e47556957e00c87e265ca934c361a5f5941b6419.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=2$ is a node.\n- $y=-2$ is a sink.\n- $y=-2$ is a source." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/90/9003cc9588b0838527f018463e2077d2d81bb5930ab70c1e3c713832e207bd99.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-2$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "4301353b-f307-5ad7-9fa6-573ee6bd3b91"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414155 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a8a7f75-ccd9-5462-8bb7-2c7b1be60406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b5/b5e7a0272bb45b9d83ec8864f58cf3b96cbb6399d079f23d168d8de5416a1c23.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414141} {:answer/id #uuid "5bd318dd-9201-58a9-b18d-379eb2818d97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8d/8d608e5ab231af991d4c55ca863e95557faae55bf44ad0e8e99f7cd0417531f2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414142} {:answer/id #uuid "80ba1f07-d31e-5ece-ad1d-7de624148041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/3e/3ed74c49c2218d24dd5235f6a2f6df948d14a58edd6872ccef17ea0434c41c2b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414146} {:answer/id #uuid "b82bf18a-7b1c-5303-9140-3233520ff812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9a/9a24e2ecc59eb8d152790c6850403f74fe9193cc9e3dd9f3d6ff6f38e494752d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414148} {:answer/id #uuid "bb6b5c8a-efde-53a2-864f-7808884654c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ae/ae926b04f1a8bb8c22cd72208b6416aa443e864083dbe4d8c2c737c049ead4f8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414149}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414141} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c0eb52d-5859-5f17-a2af-047313c84ebc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "57d5be79-8453-52cc-b08d-ee25cf4792e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-98812" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-3$ is a sink\n\n- $y=0$ is a source\n\n- $y=3$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $3$ equilibrium points, $y=-3,$ $y=0,$ and $y=3.$\n\n- We're given that $y=-3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=-3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=-2$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it (already drawn by the above property).\n\n- We're given that $y=3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it (already drawn by the above property).\n\n![](images/a4/a4d0e0f44f77e0d29d6d226b058806e947b38f4784dc61ea609ac1040b73d7dd.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](images/b5/b5e7a0272bb45b9d83ec8864f58cf3b96cbb6399d079f23d168d8de5416a1c23.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-3,$ $y=0,$ and $y=3.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-3$ appear to converge toward this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=3$ appear to converge toward this line."} :step/id #uuid "4bcfd21f-7077-56a5-b180-c56af3baefcb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414154}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414156 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362326 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c7472dd-4374-53b0-ab00-c75e67df452a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y=3$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361116} {:answer/id #uuid "7a5472b2-d6d6-52ea-a044-09bb2a1127bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y=3$ is a node" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361376} {:answer/id #uuid "858d2406-7a62-530d-b3f3-7ae4483ae919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y=3$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361434} {:answer/id #uuid "880f5f32-2604-52b9-a6aa-0210178b0d4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y=3$ is a source and $y=-3$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361442} {:answer/id #uuid "94cbc50d-4636-5057-ad39-889859bff52e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y=3$ is a sink and $y=-3$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361497}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361434} :answer-field/id #uuid "431fc151-9f48-50ea-8f92-91e1f1278462" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f9765a7a-f1b5-599a-907d-e5751bd4bc1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-98761" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=(y-3)({y}^{2}+3y+9)?$" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ (y-3)({y}^{2}+3y+9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solution is $y=3.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y-3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| ${y}^{2}+3y+9$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (3,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,3).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](images/24/244a46b17ad69e8d7cc3ae4f3d9c3c46c1d6e6a7ea154b849946f8ece6030071.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solution:\n\n- $y=3$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=3$ is a source\"."} :step/id #uuid "4cca875a-cd23-5293-b9eb-53796fc6ca53" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414158}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414157 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61206ccb-548f-5100-91ee-609c064a5202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414143} {:answer/id #uuid "69c30199-4328-5a15-bfcf-b46f5467a96f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414144} {:answer/id #uuid "6a4eb0a3-0396-53b2-9786-1fe15d9dc42d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414145} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf91e35-e89f-544d-aac8-8bed29bb7a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414147} {:answer/id #uuid "ec014581-93eb-5649-9c32-a31c943ef63c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414150}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414145} :answer-field/id #uuid "d80f5512-0801-5f44-8705-04bc06235191" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "68af5d21-8052-5193-93c5-650cda389ee6" :question/math-academy-id "q-96560" :question/problem "![](images/93/936d303dcc89c5c27e77fa3d7cfc8b63643284d9e203f176c9e0c5588c27e049.png)\n\nConsiderer the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=1$ is a node.\n- $y=-2$ is a sink.\n- $y=3$ is a source." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/69/69e895d4091075f6d50eb9e44dc027c342d1fe7ca0269224c9b6a019ab312c77.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=1$ is a source.\n\n- Statement II is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-2$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement III is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=3$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "d28d673b-fc35-5482-abfa-3f1e7616614a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414156}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414158 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "037a7d4f-c46b-5ef5-9f12-e499f543ede1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/31/31519aa878fd6a9b450296552afd9bbf628e30db68a2d7c6dc6b02a48ae28b06.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360845} {:answer/id #uuid "0562a68a-9113-579a-9191-136bc8b91243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a3/a375a537b104f5bcdd8f5951277bce2494d8861a7212772a7d7050fa14091cb9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360849} {:answer/id #uuid "2f9fb8ad-c027-5308-86ad-a2260380cc77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d7/d72110682c1959f10546b6fa49d6e4ebedf6b6d5557639c772f28a920f306df5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361050} {:answer/id #uuid "3b5da285-1e73-5eac-bb63-ee605aca7f27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/41/41f32a097325dbe2a278da2abe4ef93b13d058ffe49431882fade3bc40bc0f48.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361109} {:answer/id #uuid "bc463e5f-25db-5272-b846-75dffcfba6f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c7/c7895d3f3db254736c4e20ff30dbe92fb7c0786291b1198ed3f727971268be31.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361677}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361109} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd49cbaa-d57f-5e0e-9cec-db71bf257efe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a2a6368a-0d2f-5f5f-a7f7-7f9694dc9333" :question/math-academy-id "q-98774" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=0$ is a source\n\n- $y=-4$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=0$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=0$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-4$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=-4$ (already drawn by the above property) and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n![](images/e5/e5ba765b9caffab904f07c5086d3206f72e36e66cb018673ce7ed44d79d19e88.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](images/41/41f32a097325dbe2a278da2abe4ef93b13d058ffe49431882fade3bc40bc0f48.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=0$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=-4$ appear to converge toward this line."} :step/id #uuid "dc1c1dd6-77b7-596c-aadc-9bd547e58f0f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414155}}] :activity/title "Classifying Equilibrium Solutions of First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414140}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129971} :activity/id #uuid "606b524c-331d-50d0-b61a-4fc9da0cc19e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048275 :topic/id #uuid "8f0d9035-d5d2-5de8-a6aa-5bfd30e675ea" :topic/math-academy-id 2015} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129971 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130350 :tutorial/content "It's possible to define matrices with complex entries, such as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1+\\text{i} & 4-\\text{i} \\\\ 3+2\\text{i} & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIn general, $\\mathbb{C}^{m \\times n}$ is the space of complex matrices with dimension $m \\times n.$\n\nComplex matrices work mostly the same as real matrices (we can add them, multiply them, etc). However, complex matrices have a few extra properties:\n\n- We take the complex conjugate of a matrix by taking the complex conjugate of each component:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1+\\text{i} & 4-\\text{i} \\\\ 3+2\\text{i} & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Complex matrices also have a real part and an imaginary part. That means we can write any matrix in the form $A={\\color{blue}B}+\\textrm{i}\\,{\\color{red}C},$ where ${\\color{blue}B} = {\\color{blue}\\textrm{Re}(A)}$ and ${\\color{red}C} = {\\color{red}\\textrm{Im}(A)}.$\n\nThe real part of the matrix contains the real part of each entry, and the imaginary part of the matrix contains the imaginary part of each entry:\n \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1+\\text{i} & 4-\\text{i} \\\\ 3+2\\text{i} & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ed2b9640-b192-5de0-b784-2aad4cb89984" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3591 :tutorial/title "Matrices Over the Complex Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "4c310c92-8b22-50ab-98aa-2611b0502ab8" :step/math-academy-id 20159 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130012}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129972 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129132 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128943 :question/id #uuid "6bba4758-678a-5bf6-b9df-cdc2662ab306" :question/math-academy-id "e-5525"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c28b8ae-71ef-5d26-895a-5d949fc5b164" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393591}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Components of the Real and Imaginary Parts of a Complex Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "8f1dc324-508c-5aeb-b5fa-002f72751b63" :step/math-academy-id 20160 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130011}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129973 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129133 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128944 :question/id #uuid "0f32e78f-a13d-5e81-ba64-bff28a61aacf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5526"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef2e7753-83f4-5826-a04f-89616d34c578" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049120}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423160}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Row Operations to a Complex Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "5282e2f0-4384-5d40-a566-3bd80bc243b0" :step/math-academy-id 20161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130011 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129156 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128974 :question/id #uuid "27f2c303-b9a9-5337-bcea-865a9273b35b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5902"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83fad21d-6b36-5198-a13f-362d34342475" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393598}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Complex Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "ba63f810-d731-59ef-9355-05e269d51c2c" :step/math-academy-id 21666 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129973}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130012 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129157 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128975 :question/id #uuid "e8bc731b-bb0b-5a07-89d9-6acc9beebc82" :question/math-academy-id "e-5903"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7c1b7ad-e4bf-55b2-8665-2cf35e402946" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048896}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345171}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Complex Conjugate of a Complex Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "5542ff65-c283-5776-a3e6-a07c95c8db7d" :step/math-academy-id 21667 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129972}}] :activity/title "Matrices Over the Complex Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129358}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120676} :activity/id #uuid "045805f2-d7d9-501d-8e4b-541e2bb65e50" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048315 :topic/id #uuid "93e6a7ce-2e63-5dea-bbbb-a00575db5770" :topic/math-academy-id 1852} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121035 :tutorial/content "Given two vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ the set of all possible linear combinations of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n\n$$\nU = \\left\\{ x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\: : \\: x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe set $U$ is also called the **linear span** of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and is denoted by $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$ We say that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are the **generators** of $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the linear span generated by the following vectors:\n![](images/55/55c749dccc54c252b9120f148e383f395c09fb4cd4bec0e1cce8ed441ecb6199.svg)\n\nFrom the definition of linear span, we know that if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ then $\\mathbf{b}$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = x_1\\mathbf{a_1} + x_2\\mathbf{a_2}\n$$\n\n for some $x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the linear span of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n![](images/21/2181b471f34906eeaea7889abbba37c797a2f9624cee455b43b00fc048247cd1.svg)\n\nGeometrically, $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ can be viewed as the plane $\\Pi$ that passes through the origin and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5b202475e0bc3ebb07df1f07bab72e9e74b13a99196c2a8a5adb624a7534a03.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c4e2e270-f1fb-5633-9239-4696e8f0a25b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3193 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "05c77385-415b-5833-b935-9be3a2038c69" :step/math-academy-id 17469 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120677}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120677 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119808 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119619 :question/id #uuid "d5a81b39-421e-55c3-90de-d9f4e6489cd5" :question/math-academy-id "e-4795"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaefbc53-9df9-54a0-a969-66e062553d5c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048499}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421530}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Lies in the Span of Given Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "74f8be2d-0f86-52de-8277-fd7db395c80d" :step/math-academy-id 17471 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120750}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121036 :tutorial/content "The linear span of vectors can be interpreted geometrically as the points in space that can be reached by adding multiples of those vectors.\n\n- The span of a single nonzero vector is a *line* through the origin. More precisely, if $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ then $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a} \\}$ determines the *line* with direction vector $\\mathbf{a}$ that passes through the origin. For instance, if\n![](images/dd/ddef437718d3a11811da02a17e2207d75e2d1852eeada67ab494af45b2a655e2.svg)\nthen $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a} \\}$ represents the line $l$ *spanned* by $\\mathbf{a}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/08/080aa6203494e915e28c491a3d86176f7ba634bcbdfe40b094c07d85fb29bb69.png)\n\n- The span of two non-parallel vectors is a *plane* through the origin. More precisely, if $\\mathbf{a} \\not\\parallel \\mathbf{b}$ ($\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel), then $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\}$ determines the *plane* with direction vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ that passes through the origin. For instance, if\n![](images/1f/1f92378ff1d1409f0928caa4b3ce98861784d0f2aa51dd1eda30e8877a0134ff.svg)\nthen $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\}$  represents the plane $\\Pi$ *spanned* by $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/56/5606535bd91eb35745a9852ceb451720339105f5a97fb66ccfcb1164ecd55ffd.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c50c93be-7bf2-5511-b456-c60478a53270" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3194 :tutorial/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Linear Span"} :step/id #uuid "15713f7b-cd11-55c3-93fd-8040ac62d36f" :step/math-academy-id 17477 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120679}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119809 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119620 :question/id #uuid "94a17888-dcef-58e0-8147-0dec3745f136" :question/math-academy-id "e-4799"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a04b551f-3323-5b91-b01e-941912cc92be" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050308}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421528}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Lies on a Plane"} :step/id #uuid "33abdb88-235d-550f-b5bb-06946454437c" :step/math-academy-id 17478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120750 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119856 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119671 :question/id #uuid "21a6808a-5b1c-5b1d-9bd4-da605fb6d920" :question/math-academy-id "e-6218"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fac58a79-2d3f-5ad3-b1ab-c5ce279c4ebe" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421532}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Unknown Such That a Vector Lies in the Span of Given Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "5cca50af-2a48-5696-bc3a-fc57e95232b0" :step/math-academy-id 23264 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120678}}] :activity/title "Linear Span of Vectors in N-Dimensional Euclidean Space" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120046}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158117} :activity/id #uuid "93123186-af04-50ed-9f1a-970323c9acfd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048681 :topic/id #uuid "b45261a2-56f4-554a-ba13-6946f5dc3f8c" :topic/math-academy-id 2828} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158113 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157284 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157088 :question/id #uuid "35acfa60-3395-5f11-94f9-627499386932" :question/math-academy-id "e-7159"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9a9b6de-b0dd-5b38-a32b-378d2b1d86a2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368561}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Difference of Sets Without a Venn Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "ca424c53-0556-5e18-9bc0-d39fdd10d96b" :step/math-academy-id 26359 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158114}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158114 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157285 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157089 :question/id #uuid "89c94a89-21de-5a4f-a365-85701b2e4ef2" :question/math-academy-id "e-7160"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67e90c94-5018-582f-8692-a1155f41f7c7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368798}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Differences of Sets Given Verbally"} :step/id #uuid "94c5e547-ed98-5f00-a17f-fb87e8db7939" :step/math-academy-id 26360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158117 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158474 :tutorial/content "The **difference** of two sets $A$ and $B,$ denoted by $A \\setminus B$ or $A - B,$ is the set of all elements belonging to $A$ and *not* belonging to $B.$\n\nFor example, consider the sets $A = \\{ a,b,c \\}$ and $B = \\{ b, d, e \\}.$ We can represent these sets using a Venn diagram, as follows:\n\n![](images/4a/4a2d3be8c64b5527dcb37ac68d99cd1e36009725d0847597c5bbd417bbd5f7ad.png)\n\nThe difference $A \\setminus B$ consists of all the elements that are in $A$ but not $B.$ To represent $A \\setminus B,$ we will shade in all the area that is contained within $A$ but not $B.$\n\n![](images/49/49743d4647817294c2287bc15a496fb5d6c73070ed52cfd8934d1babe93c1b25.png)\n\nThe elements $a$ and $c$ are contained in $A$ but not $B.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA \\setminus B = A - B = \\{ a,c \\}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The difference can be defined using set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA \\setminus B = A - B = \\{ x \\mid x \\in A \\text { and } x \\notin B \\}\n$$\n\nThe condition \"$x \\in A \\text { and } x \\notin B$\" means that $x$ must belong to $A$ but $x$ must *not* belong to $B.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "48529a2b-cc59-57e8-8624-78fd4339f88c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4333 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1e4b9023-ad62-55c8-950f-2d1e0371a65e" :step/math-academy-id 26364 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158122}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158122 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157289 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157093 :question/id #uuid "9e0d2770-a884-558f-becb-a38fe709b2a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-7167"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4687dcd7-a5ab-5dc6-be6d-89d806a61a02" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049065}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354498}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Difference of Sets Using a Venn Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "46adbc13-f6f2-55a5-861d-63519ad07f7b" :step/math-academy-id 26372 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158129}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158129 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158480 :tutorial/content "Although Venn diagrams can be useful to visualize differences, we do not have to draw a Venn diagram every time we wish to compute a difference.\n\nFor example, let's again consider the sets $A = \\{ a,b,c \\}$ and $B = \\{ b, d, e \\}.$ This time, we will compute the difference $A \\setminus B$ without using a Venn diagram. We just need to find all the elements that are in $A$ but not $B.$\n\nSo, we will go through the elements of $A$ and check if each element is also in $B.$ Remember that we are looking for elements that are *not* in $B.$\n\n- For the element $a \\in A,$ we have $a \\not\\in B. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- For the element $b \\in A,$ we have $b \\in B. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- For the element $c \\in A,$ we have $c \\not\\in B. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nThe elements of $A$ that are not in $B$ are $a$ and $c.$\n\nTherefore, the difference is\n\n$$\nA \\setminus B = A - B = \\{ a,c \\}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "14afef0e-fec6-5cdf-82c4-98391973df49" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4342 :tutorial/title "Differences Without Venn Diagrams"} :step/id #uuid "2ef8179b-8279-55d0-b781-5afd8ba23248" :step/math-academy-id 26397 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158113}}] :activity/title "The Difference of Sets" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157485}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162974} :activity/id #uuid "9110c851-c32a-57df-a77a-329797a3df41" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048731 :topic/id #uuid "b99b224f-8118-53d5-8d6e-c47785040b57" :topic/math-academy-id 3028} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162971 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162056 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161856 :question/id #uuid "01fc4e2e-d93f-50b3-859e-19732d4f10c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9111"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "405984cd-1e1b-54c6-bc75-268e127b9812" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050327}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420139}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Constant"} :step/id #uuid "3174d04a-7e8e-5098-91d3-c55afedd2e39" :step/math-academy-id 33959 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162975}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162972 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162057 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161857 :question/id #uuid "a3f88c0e-9426-50d2-baf0-95909e222d4b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9112"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a3752a0-431d-5520-97b1-493c36bbce54" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435371}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Scaled Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "fe3d0b73-cb7e-5370-9455-c490da334890" :step/math-academy-id 33961 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162976}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162973 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162058 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161858 :question/id #uuid "c20c7b15-e967-52a9-846d-4915f6661158" :question/math-academy-id "e-9114"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85388f7d-b2ee-520b-b711-a7721c6f620c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425505}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Transformed Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "09f12104-7949-5867-9884-48e422a32857" :step/math-academy-id 33963 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162977}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162974 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163273 :tutorial/content "There are several important properties of the variance of a random variable that we can use to simplify expressions. \n\nFirstly, the variance of a constant is always zero. That is, for any constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[a] = 0.\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this makes sense. A constant does not vary, so the mean deviation from its expected value should be zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "6eb17ec7-1e57-5d75-8a71-9030a420602b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5458 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "65bbda75-37ec-5809-926a-0fb8d31c008d" :step/math-academy-id 33964 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162971}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162975 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163274 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $X$ is a random variable and $a$ is a constant. Then,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nSo, for example, if we know that $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3X] & ={3}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =9\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[-4X] & =(-4)^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =16\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To understand why we have to square the constant when we factor it outside the variance, it's helpful to remember that the variance is defined as the expected *squared* deviation from the mean:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ (X - \\textrm E[X])^2 ]\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[aX] & =\\text{E}[(aX-\\text{E}[aX])^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(aX-a\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{a}^{2}(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d33b6982-066e-5c82-8138-0283ad8dccbb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5459 :tutorial/title "The Variance of a Scaled Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "55f9fd86-1f4a-5f2c-b7c2-a60113a4a9e8" :step/math-academy-id 33966 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162972}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162976 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163275 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $X$ is a random variable and $a$ and $b$ are constants. It can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX+b] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nA proof of this result is given at the end of this lesson.\n\nTherefore, when a random variable is scaled by a factor of $a$ and shifted by a constant $b\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- the variance is scaled by a factor of $a^2$ as before, and\n\n- the variance is unaffected by the shift.\n\nThe fact that shifting a random variable leaves the variance unaltered makes intuitive sense. Shifting all values of $X$ by a fixed amount does not change how far apart those values are from each other. \n\nFor example, if we know that $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2X+1] & ={2}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1523e51b-84da-5100-809f-09fbcb0d80ba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5460 :tutorial/title "The Variance of a Transformed Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "e17beec2-91ed-5375-9c58-ab279784f2bf" :step/math-academy-id 33968 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162973}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162977 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163276 :tutorial/content "We wish to prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX+b] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X]\n$$\n\nRecall that, by definition,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ (X - \\textrm E[X])^2 ].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[aX+b] & =\\text{E}[(aX+b-\\text{E}[aX+b])^{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expected value, we can simplify this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[(aX+b-\\text{E}[aX+b])^{2}] & =\\text{E}[(aX+b-a\\text{E}[X]-b)^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(aX-a\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(a(X-\\text{E}[X]))^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{a}^{2}(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0372f0de-b6a6-58ee-8195-c04024e8f745" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5461 :tutorial/title "Proof of the General Formula"} :step/id #uuid "da23e59c-3020-5db2-8f5b-77675938bf0b" :step/math-academy-id 33969}] :activity/title "Properties of Variance for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162181}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425510} :activity/id #uuid "6f0ea926-98c1-508c-aafd-6b9334df01ce" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048731 :topic/id #uuid "b99b224f-8118-53d5-8d6e-c47785040b57" :topic/math-academy-id 3028} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425507 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e9447cf-647a-5fa5-b1b7-8ad767262b04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425493} {:answer/id #uuid "26c878c9-4005-5ffb-b62a-ccf12e7653a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425495} {:answer/id #uuid "84d7e9cc-2b46-5e49-827d-3dc43ec7a3fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425499} {:answer/id #uuid "a04fede6-c5e8-5346-92ee-0dfe50e7363c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425500} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e38c85-b6da-52ad-a873-fe8715cae083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425501}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425493} :answer-field/id #uuid "9be7f666-e571-52b8-923e-291edd79be24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2adf151d-6d7b-5aee-975a-4588dddc072c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109086" :question/problem "If $\\text{Var}[X]=18,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{3}X+1].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+b]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $b=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{3}X+1] & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 18 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0925688f-b14f-5497-b2f8-ef5577ee74ea" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425511}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425508 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162267 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af8dce06-ec2e-54f0-8068-081d29a00d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158805} {:answer/id #uuid "f65b10d3-6570-596d-aef7-36a7d9e90bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158806} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c1bb8e-9e55-5585-849e-6e82c2d6778c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158807} {:answer/id #uuid "2866549e-9133-5ffd-b73b-2bb7a74f8397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158808} {:answer/id #uuid "4319aba1-1a6d-55f0-8649-0a11c8e26ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158809}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158808} :answer-field/id #uuid "96f5d0fa-e64f-5420-894c-9af20eb4994a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "07dc96f1-44f9-56f8-847d-dfc0e5291318" :question/math-academy-id "q-108946" :question/problem "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=7,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[2-5X].$ \n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+b]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=-5$ and $b=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2-5X] & =(-5)^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =25\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=7.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={5}^{2}\\cdot f(5)+{6}^{2}\\cdot f(6)+{9}^{2}\\cdot f(9) \\\\  & =25(\\frac{1}{5})+36(\\frac{2}{5})+81(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{259}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{259}{5}-{7}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{259}{5}-49 \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2-5X] & =25\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =25\\cdot \\frac{14}{5} \\\\  & =14\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1bca5d11-c663-5970-a2d7-41fbabf9a6af" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425509}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425509 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425506 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ffc334f-bda6-5020-8972-7c141e811580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425494} {:answer/id #uuid "2a01301e-2613-5381-a06c-9e16aa051fd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425496} {:answer/id #uuid "3e1bb640-287c-5a4d-9809-a95f12e541cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425497} {:answer/id #uuid "559bb6ee-9e3b-59fc-b4ec-1934f445e465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425498} {:answer/id #uuid "bea01d67-a086-5031-9b05-53086e759f6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425502}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425496} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef907e56-83d3-5832-9059-ba2b6e97be1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6da9aedf-d051-5c8d-9ea5-b796001b82b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-109056" :question/problem "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=2,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{3}X].$ \n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\sqrt{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{3}X] & ={\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=2.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={0}^{2}\\cdot f(0)+{2}^{2}\\cdot f(2)+{4}^{2}\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}+16\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{3}X] & =3\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2775e72e-c277-5c4c-8f6b-b21b5d18a293" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425507}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425510 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162264 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdc2e9bd-883b-53b3-9944-cfc609ec7c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158787} {:answer/id #uuid "1da0e3f6-cfb8-53e6-a30c-128f6ea47744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158788} {:answer/id #uuid "456a4994-4a3b-5692-99e8-a647708d7a6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158789} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc8dced-9c70-5f0e-a44a-554c2928171a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158790} {:answer/id #uuid "57a37707-7dd6-55aa-bf77-c3c838e9d2a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158791}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158787} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a78dcdb-24b3-5b74-9b4c-2a39c0c6e272" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1375f84f-9c97-5d6b-9ab0-2b5843b7a89e" :question/math-academy-id "q-108936" :question/problem "If $\\text{Var}[X]=8,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[2X].$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2X] & ={2}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =4\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "53444b7f-e4a7-573c-87a7-c9522567fb10" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425508}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425511 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37f0111e-003f-5538-a3b8-ed1430d08fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420111} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f2c3dd-df0a-57f1-9b19-b251ffe9a72e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420122} {:answer/id #uuid "bc26c2f9-39d9-5339-a3e8-be5d2e945ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420123} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d13b79-c01f-5ddb-888f-f826b5a1451c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420124} {:answer/id #uuid "e83e0132-215d-5133-98be-6554e8873e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420128}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420128} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d0a832f-24dd-571f-b252-8ec9cfdfbd85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "12feee0e-318a-5930-8eca-dd111ec7b1c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-109091" :question/problem "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=1,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{2}X].$ \n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+b]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $b=\\frac{1}{8}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{2}X] & ={(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=1.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={0}^{2}\\cdot f(0)+{1}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{3}^{2}\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+9\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{2}X] & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e8ab5da7-93a7-56c3-964a-5c487de36ed5"}] :activity/title "Properties of Variance for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425492}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435373} :activity/id #uuid "25dbc02e-bef9-5aef-9959-0f81fd1d0280" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048731 :topic/id #uuid "b99b224f-8118-53d5-8d6e-c47785040b57" :topic/math-academy-id 3028} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435372 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "643b59ab-e862-5975-86dd-7355eed4cca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242081} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1ac25e-ba63-509b-9370-23e96efe4ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242163} {:answer/id #uuid "bac7e8e7-11c4-554e-b61e-02f375d7783a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242819} {:answer/id #uuid "dc315568-edd6-5abe-b917-d1c33d5e4173" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243136} {:answer/id #uuid "e1fe8786-4596-56fa-ad58-012b52d63167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243186}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243186} :answer-field/id #uuid "718f5cfd-3b86-5662-9124-8b1ae998dc61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7bc237e8-7011-5d1b-897c-e565fd160016" :question/math-academy-id "q-109090" :question/problem "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=0,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4].$ \n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+b]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\sqrt{5}$ and $b=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4] & ={(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=0.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & =(-1)^{2}\\cdot f(-1)+{0}^{2}\\cdot f(0)+{1}^{2}\\cdot f(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}-{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4] & =5\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "02fca583-999d-5fea-95bb-2fa89ae7e5ac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435374}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435373 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "039f84c9-15b6-5535-9aa7-17f63520b5ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435365} {:answer/id #uuid "31713117-0676-52eb-93ae-63c695655871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435366} {:answer/id #uuid "7d294bd2-f457-5854-b801-d01d068966a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435367} {:answer/id #uuid "a9534937-1eca-5636-899b-550bfbfe6e07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435368} {:answer/id #uuid "fc28d66e-a0b9-5d76-9c20-a4f103450187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435369}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435367} :answer-field/id #uuid "aadf43d0-7c01-553a-93ba-140bd9fcc568" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a7e780b8-35dc-5693-8651-c458793347c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-109059" :question/problem "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=1,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[-2X].$ \n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[-2X] & =(-2)^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=1.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={0}^{2}\\cdot f(0)+{1}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{3}^{2}\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+9\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[-2X] & =4\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "142fd15b-c875-518a-9165-bb478aa0e810" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435372}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435374 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369651 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a7a743c-110e-5e17-9b8e-c0a4ed4bf822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369653} {:answer/id #uuid "d0bc9a45-3558-56aa-b44c-19b91bed820a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369654} {:answer/id #uuid "6840aa01-0203-5583-a1fb-3ee0ba2fced3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369655} {:answer/id #uuid "53b84c0f-bed5-541b-8d3e-723a5410d69e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369656} {:answer/id #uuid "288b9421-5232-599d-975c-4cacf3b01709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369657}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369655} :answer-field/id #uuid "e907f6da-cd04-5106-a3c9-cf468f523d2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aeeea4a5-1443-5d67-a950-8990e0faa656" :question/math-academy-id "q-129386" :question/problem "If $\\text{Var}[X]=3.6,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[\\frac{10}{3}X].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{10}{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\frac{10}{3}X] & ={(\\frac{10}{3})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{9}\\cdot 3.6 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "98fbfae8-3ff5-5189-8ae4-c342310ec549"}] :activity/title "Properties of Variance for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435364}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120707} :activity/id #uuid "28cf8e1a-d07a-556d-a006-2d022a4740c2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048850 :topic/id #uuid "c60f74c0-bf82-529d-b9dd-b8bfe8e2cc4a" :topic/math-academy-id 1799} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120707 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121048 :tutorial/content "Suppose we know that the vector equation of the line $l$ is given by\n![](images/92/92c5ed55727717320b9c128adf1a6d786666adb38cd6ba71d57917e60d661fd8.svg)\n\nand we want to rewrite it using Cartesian coordinates. In other words, we want to create an equation that relates $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ to each other.\n\nWe can do this using the following $3$ steps.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the parametric equations for the line.\n\nSubstituting ![](images/67/67b8360f90540ffb75d39717c375f0ef6e9a8a39cae5832c78957105e0d148b6.svg) into the equation of $l,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3+6t \\\\ -2+5t \\\\ 5-t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3+6t \\\\ y=-2+5t \\\\ z=5-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Solve for $t$ in each of the parametric equations.\n\n![](images/ee/eed0a210b121af73bc2ceab04b95def59239a742055284e706a1532b47edc60d.svg)\n\n**Step 3:** Equate the resulting expressions. Because the right-hand side of each equation is equal to $t,$ we can write this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-3}{6}=\\dfrac{y+2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-5}{-1} = t\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by omitting the \"$=t$\" on the right-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-3}{6}=\\dfrac{y+2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-5}{-1} \\,.\n$$\n\nAnd we are done! \n\nThis way of writing the equation of a line is called the **Cartesian equation** or **Cartesian canonical equation** (or simply **canonical equation**).\n\nIn general, the Cartesian equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-x_0}{v_x}=\\dfrac{y-y_0}{v_y}=\\dfrac{z-z_0}{v_z} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4b2a904a-eda3-58e1-9b0f-e742454f48b6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3245 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "762cbf4d-a393-51df-98bb-da96c034848b" :step/math-academy-id 17885 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120708}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120708 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119826 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119637 :question/id #uuid "005f8f3f-89eb-5e99-bf0f-82b67329661a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4910"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9927bff1-c245-542b-8d5d-fa7d24188b85" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048729}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413273}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given in Canonical Form"} :step/id #uuid "0e305d64-a5b0-5e37-85c9-271d9bd1785b" :step/math-academy-id 17886 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120709}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120709 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119827 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119638 :question/id #uuid "897aa930-02ca-5f79-95d0-5ee11420da97" :question/math-academy-id "e-4911"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44cf726-a4a6-5ac9-a63c-7464fe5566c5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413270}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given in Parametric or Vector Form"} :step/id #uuid "15d988ae-4b19-5d9f-a7e2-fd6fd8c3f4c6" :step/math-academy-id 17887 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120728}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120710 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119828 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119639 :question/id #uuid "df314d1d-56c8-5503-b632-8a425b47070f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4912"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc74baa-313b-51e7-8ae1-5e9fe0761256" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413267}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given Two Points on the Line"} :step/id #uuid "1e9d9f49-2eb3-5745-9da4-70a87dddd9f6" :step/math-academy-id 17888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120728 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121055 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to write the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n![](images/9d/9d4a6d56170de2e0dbfa7e2a6f51905b19f9ada9fa1900aafe2672f54831be86.svg)\n\nUsing the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-3}{0} = t,\t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),\n$$\n\nor equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-3}{0}.\n$$\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because $\\dfrac{z-3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/0b/0bea89e098af6be643450df08d30b97c0c3d658075b0b891ab2c4c7f37077f42.svg)\n\nIf we solve for $t$ in the parametric equations of the line, we get\n\n![](images/f3/f399dae55ae8c720f7769fa78a42b30b2a7b7aab49ed1515d9c43d371f3a9f71.svg)\n\nOnly the first two equations have right-hand sides equal to $t,$ so we combine them but keep the third equation separate:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}, \\; z=3\n$$\n\nSo, it's as if we removed the $\\dfrac{z-3}{0}$ part in the original formula and replaced it with $z=3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7a2d263d-c94f-5d86-859a-c6c79c835eca" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3316 :tutorial/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero"} :step/id #uuid "c618a49e-3866-523c-b010-33f9b474436e" :step/math-academy-id 18457 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120729}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120729 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119842 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119656 :question/id #uuid "74fb2af1-d791-5687-98cc-0b2db5bd8464" :question/math-academy-id "e-5021"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07c209a4-5c69-561e-9015-28b0f6e436e5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413272}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero"} :step/id #uuid "669d1115-ca36-5052-810d-cb802269d1fa" :step/math-academy-id 18458 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120710}}] :activity/title "The Cartesian Equation of a Line" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120015}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116304} :activity/id #uuid "ec8f8b4c-f624-58ca-8d08-26dd958651aa" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049186 :topic/id #uuid "e1c10e80-3811-5fcd-98a3-8118c49347e4" :topic/math-academy-id 1737} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116304 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116549 :tutorial/content "The domain of a vector-valued function $\\mathbf f(t)$ is the set of values of $t$ for which the function is well-defined. \n\nA function is well-defined at some value $t = t_0$ if all its component functions are defined at $t_0.$ Thus, the domain of $\\mathbf f$ is the *intersection* of the domains of its component functions.\n\nLet's find the domain of the following function:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t) = \\dfrac{1}{3+t} \\: \\mathbf i + t^2 \\: \\mathbf j + \\sqrt{-t} \\: \\mathbf k\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the domains of the component functions:\n\n- The domain of $\\dfrac{1}{3+t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,-3) \\cup (-3,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of $t^2$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of $\\sqrt{-t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty, 0].$\n\nTo find the domain of $\\mathbf{f},$ we need the intersection of the component functions' domains. This intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7c/7cf7270cc873e9de1fedb7bd413bba1f9ed1f7cd604b3ae640d32d9e0b41bdef.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,-3) \\cup (-3,0].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d01e4be3-25c3-5922-9f88-c8eb7538e62e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7628 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6c2c6552-011f-50fb-b06a-06bc2262ccf3" :step/math-academy-id 44256 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116305}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116305 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115354 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114917 :question/id #uuid "9a86e942-8852-5cce-b9be-e7a5eff46d12" :question/math-academy-id "e-12350"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "461c0f55-cb85-511e-b8cb-be11f8a3c250" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050642}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365178}] :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Reciprocal and Radical Components"} :step/id #uuid "a07d4a92-4626-5d50-92cc-ea8d55c5707d" :step/math-academy-id 44257 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116306}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116306 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115355 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114918 :question/id #uuid "726fa7f3-a110-5730-a734-1019fd77de9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12362"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a1499c-080d-5008-bcc3-492ef469d3dc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050829}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434608}] :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Exponential and Logarithmic Components"} :step/id #uuid "cfc9c5f1-5579-598f-a9e2-d23b659763f7" :step/math-academy-id 44297 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116307}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116307 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115356 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114919 :question/id #uuid "a63854e6-412b-5308-be28-c05337aa4b9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12455"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "553f7b9d-c387-59b0-a0d6-9624d012e414" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049372}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420093}] :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Trigonometric Components"} :step/id #uuid "3947d2bf-1e5f-5028-8a1b-43cfaacc4546" :step/math-academy-id 44801}] :activity/title "The Domain of a Vector-Valued Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115482}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411595} :activity/id #uuid "96cfc23a-e860-5cec-be62-9f9b3c48eb57" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049186 :topic/id #uuid "e1c10e80-3811-5fcd-98a3-8118c49347e4" :topic/math-academy-id 1737} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411591 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfa1654c-e948-5fc3-a89a-4fe52cc8fccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi n}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398578} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1b7514-50b8-5ddd-9dc3-5b90007cd7cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi n}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398579} {:answer/id #uuid "61124a49-e750-5eed-9f63-4fab70f48c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398580} {:answer/id #uuid "282a04d7-5121-553a-8c70-66602f8c83db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi n}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398581} {:answer/id #uuid "4a1c8270-8fd2-50fc-8447-30acc60e9312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi n}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398582}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398578} :answer-field/id #uuid "4112b8f8-a1d3-5ca1-a272-50f00bc81e5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b2987e81-f85b-5262-9ea2-835ea44d86e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162644" :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=(t+1)\\,i+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t)\\,j+{e}^{-t}\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\bbox[5pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $t+1$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t)$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ except for the values of $t$ for which\n\n$$\n3t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi n}{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component ${e}^{-t}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi n}{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer."} :step/id #uuid "3ce5a87f-f4ad-59bf-8bf7-3a76cd4d166e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411594}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411592 :step/content {:db/id 