 2nd component ${e}^{2s}$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}}$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7a/7aacfa509e2e642fd90ef989e4dec3a2f9f5ad1e8def55f894b88d97bcabb971.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)$ is $s\\in (-2,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$"} :step/id #uuid "8fb273d6-8594-5f60-8d7f-4364fd17bdf4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434612}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281523 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d70cc6b-bd49-5fad-80dd-107c44a59a26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\in (1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278991} {:answer/id #uuid "39fdb5cd-6cc3-529f-b505-53703b5e20d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\in (-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279104} {:answer/id #uuid "4e468aef-35d1-5ca8-9435-b946edd08ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\in (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279302} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3e9b8a-bee0-59ae-acb6-e94db4a1db28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\in (1,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279711} {:answer/id #uuid "91a8bb4c-3e02-54c8-ba2f-ada2f9a6fe60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\in (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279890}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279302} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b67c2d1-20af-5e46-8732-2ab3a65c3c67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7c1f4ba8-733f-52ec-ba54-2cc86581e097" :question/math-academy-id "q-162661" :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)=⟨\\frac{1}{s-1},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡s,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s-2)⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components. \n\n- The domain of $\\frac{1}{s-1}$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡s$ is $s\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s-2)$ is  $s\\in (2,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/ac/ac5fed5129673869c7b9e0da3bd46f224aedbfda11b3a26e507b533d66c4e4e9.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)$ is $s\\in (2,\\infty ).$"} :step/id #uuid "ab7de720-165e-5d5d-a64a-1a64ae913cfe"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11188107-8128-5947-9d14-6b2ff56a2513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278748} {:answer/id #uuid "792a4a98-c522-5228-85fc-5aac6d5cff61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279673} {:answer/id #uuid "bffccb23-963c-523b-b10b-709940b33bf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280281} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf765d7-ed5e-524a-a27b-5d8678c4f142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280409} {:answer/id #uuid "f639275c-6781-5bf6-8ee3-3e36faf90d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280749}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280409} :answer-field/id #uuid "5306462d-65c3-5c3f-b61d-973703c8ad19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "540767cb-cd6f-5ce4-bc0f-7ec2c2e165a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162636" :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\,i+(t+1)^{2}\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\bbox[5pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$, $t\\ne n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $(t+1)^{2}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer."} :step/id #uuid "d102d3e6-4a8f-5810-a213-fcf495285bef" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434610}}] :activity/title "The Domain of a Vector-Valued Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434601}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084378} :activity/id #uuid "df562fd2-75a6-537c-95d0-fc56957ff191" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049947 :topic/id #uuid "20890d14-bf28-5a28-925d-52b10bb4e884" :topic/math-academy-id 762} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084378 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084389 :tutorial/content "For a particle moving along a straight line with velocity $v(t),$ we calculate the acceleration $a(t)$ by differentiating $v$ with respect to $t.$ But what if we're given the acceleration and want to find the velocity?\n\nWe need to do the reverse operation of differentiation, which is integration. So, we integrate $a(t)$ with respect to $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\na(t) = \\frac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad v(t) = \\int a(t)\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe determine the arbitrary constant of integration using some information that's known about the system." :tutorial/id #uuid "b49f3f0b-5b5d-542c-91c4-19be6581120d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1009 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "100f62e7-30d3-53a3-8066-4c5757c6b8fd" :step/math-academy-id 617656 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084379}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084379 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083393 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083010 :question/id #uuid "7fc6630d-00e2-5528-a1d5-4c7f7b43e8a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-562"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee2920dc-93e1-5595-a751-62ad5e4f3035" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050901}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394242}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Given By a Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "43253f98-3564-58be-b53d-0b7c73ec32e5" :step/math-academy-id 617657 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084380}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084380 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083394 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083013 :question/id #uuid "9a655242-0547-5a0a-b4cc-2fb3caa11584" :question/math-academy-id "e-563"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f20159e8-8f86-5bf8-b87e-3eb990b819f1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370645}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Not a Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "95e07377-0421-53ad-88e4-ea49c586cba0" :step/math-academy-id 617660}] :activity/title "Calculating Velocity Using Integration" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083451}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055553} :activity/id #uuid "8a7c3e16-7b5a-517e-b02a-29c35f81f008" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050923 :topic/id #uuid "723d1f9c-b696-511c-b5e5-88c045fe7609" :topic/math-academy-id 50} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055553 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056044 :tutorial/content "Given two sets $A$ and $B,$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B,$ we say that $A$ is a **subset** of $B,$ and we write \n\n$$\nA \\subseteq B.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{ {\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{blue}{3}} \\}, \\qquad B = \\{ 1,2,3\\}, \\qquad C=\\{1,2,{\\color{red}4}\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that *every* element of $A$ is also an element of $B{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{1}}\\in B, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{3}}\\in B\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is a subset of $B,$ and we write $A \\subseteq B.$\n\nWe can visualize the sets $A$ and $B$ as shown below. Notice that $A$ is contained entirely within $B$ (here, $U$ is the universal set).\n\n![](images/91/911dbbe9a638810f3a245fdcd004e10aee4dbd49fd8a99741c1bdd5c48cc2319.png)\n\nOn the other hand, not every element of $C$ is an element of $B{:}$\n\n$$\n1 \\in B, \\qquad 2\\in B, \\qquad {\\color{red}4}\\notin B\n$$\n\nSince $4 \\notin B,$ we have that $C$ is *not* a subset of $B,$ and we write $C \\not\\subseteq B.$\n\nWe can visualize the sets $B$ and $C$ as follows. Notice that $C$ is *not* contained entirely within $B$ (here, $U$ is the universal set).\n\n![](images/45/454c28bacb8d5117bb11f76988b70344b4521d260adf2b690a11e7dfd2964385.png)\n\nAnother way of thinking about the statement $C \\not\\subseteq B$ is that $C$ contains *at least one* element that is not an element of $B.$ In this case, that element is $4.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2ef02f03-d232-5ab1-88fc-1f363535caa3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8240 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a4c7c6c6-5b36-53d1-a4de-4c76a981a433" :step/math-academy-id 152757 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055554}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055554 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056055 :tutorial/content "We note the following points regarding subsets:\n\n- Every set $A$ is a subset of itself:\n\n$$\nA\\subseteq A\n$$\n\nThis is because $A$ contains the same elements as $A,$ and therefore $A$ is a subset of $A.$\n\n- The empty set is a subset of *every* set. So, for all sets $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\emptyset \\subseteq A.\n$$\n\nTo understand this, remember that $\\emptyset \\not\\subseteq A$ means that $\\emptyset$ contains *at least one* element that is not an element of $A.$ However, this cannot be true since $\\emptyset$ contains no elements! Thus, we must have $\\emptyset\\subseteq A.$\n\nIt's worth pointing out that the empty set is a subset of itself:\n\n$$\n\\emptyset \\subseteq \\emptyset\n$$\n\nFinally, note that a set $A$ is a **proper subset** of a set $B$ if $A \\subseteq B$ and $A \\neq B.$ In this case, we can write \n\n$$\nA \\subset B.\n$$\n\nTo explain this concretely, consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{ 1,3 \\}, \\qquad B = \\{ 1, 2, 3 \\}, \\qquad C = \\{ 3,2,1 \\}\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a proper subset of $B,$ but $C$ is *not* a proper subset of $B{:}$\n\n- Since every element of $A$ is an element of $B,$ we have that $A\\subseteq B.$ Also, $A\\neq B.$ Therefore, $A$ is a *proper* subset of $B{:}$\n\n$$\nA\\subset B\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, $C\\subseteq B$ because every element of $C$ is an element of $B.$ However, $C$ is *not* a proper subset of $B$ because $C=B.$ Therefore, we write \n\n$$\nC \\not\\subset B.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1acb16ee-d59d-5050-9848-9cfdb6e2ee6b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8316 :tutorial/title "Some Remarks"} :step/id #uuid "9cee0391-700f-550d-9b6e-139dae503e1e" :step/math-academy-id 152758 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055555}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055555 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054629 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054442 :question/id #uuid "70291b7a-ae46-5e8a-b24b-3ea343eb84f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-13195"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395707}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set"} :step/id #uuid "b812cc2f-347b-5ed3-979d-e5bff1beb134" :step/math-academy-id 152759 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055556}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055556 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056047 :tutorial/content "We can systematically list all the subsets of a given set using a tree diagram.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find all the subsets of the set $A=\\{ 0,1 \\}.$\n\nWe list every possible combination of elements within the set using a tree diagram like the one below.\n\n![](images/dd/dd51f5e02c9f280b8478e11497602dd7ae886b84d1d5994d8af73dff9719d20e.png)\n\nWe start with the empty set as the root $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\{ \\}}.$ \n\nNext, we consider the element $0\\in A.$ We can either include it or not include it in a new subset.\n\n- If we don't include $0$ in $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\{ \\}},$ we are left with the empty set $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{\\}}.$ Next, we consider the element $1\\in A.$ We can either include it or not include it in a new subset:\n\n- If we don't include $1$ in $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{\\}},$ we are left with $\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{\\}}.$\n\n- If we include $1$ in $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{\\}},$ we get $\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{1\\}}.$\n\n- If we include $0$ in $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\{ \\}},$ we get the set $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{0\\}}.$ Next, we consider the element $1\\in A.$ We can either include it or not include it in a new subset:\n\n- If we don't include $1$ in $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{0\\}},$ we are left with $\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{0\\}}.$\n\n- If we include $1$ in $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{0\\}},$ we get $\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{0,1\\}}.$\n\nFinally, the list of all subsets is shown in the last column:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{\\}},\\quad \\: \\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{0\\}},\\quad \\: \\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{1\\}},\\quad \\: \\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{0,1\\}}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "daebe910-6059-595b-a45a-fc890a9b1b53" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8244 :tutorial/title "Listing All the Subsets of a Set"} :step/id #uuid "547755c4-e17d-56bf-a674-91a455f0dbbf" :step/math-academy-id 152763 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055557}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055557 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054630 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054443 :question/id #uuid "0c50c147-4043-5aa0-b716-a9745206fdb4" :question/math-academy-id "e-13196"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbddaef-3828-5454-b892-26a3c6242ddb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408073}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding All Subsets of a Given Set"} :step/id #uuid "2e361262-f159-54e6-a942-659d5017ac7b" :step/math-academy-id 152764 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055558}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055558 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056056 :tutorial/content "Recall that the natural numbers, integers, rational numbers, and real and complex numbers can be arranged in the following hierarchical diagram.\n\n![](images/66/66260e4c239137cd30453d9335256fd7d84e93a197612d670c19d97e417bd2f0.png)\n\nIn other words, $\\mathbb{N}$ is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{Z},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{Q},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{R},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{C}.$ We can write this symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{N} \\subseteq \\mathbb{Z} \\subseteq \\mathbb{Q} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R} \\subseteq \\mathbb{C}\n$$\n\nSince none of these sets are equivalent, we can also write\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{N} \\subset \\mathbb{Z} \\subset \\mathbb{Q} \\subset \\mathbb{R} \\subset \\mathbb{C}.\n$$\n\nSome restricted or extended special sets are subsets of others. For example:\n\n- $\\mathbb N$ is the set of natural numbers, and $\\mathbb N_0$ is the set of natural numbers *and* zero. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\mathbb N\\subset \\mathbb N_0.\n$$\n\n- $\\mathbb Z$ is the set of integers, $\\mathbb Z^+$ is the set of positive integers (equivalent to $\\mathbb N$), and $\\mathbb Z^+_0$ is the set of positive integers and zero (equivalent to $\\mathbb N_0$). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Z^+\\subset  \\mathbb Z_0^+ \\subset \\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\n- $\\mathbb Q^+$ is the set of positive rational numbers, $\\mathbb R^+$ is the set of positive real numbers, and $\\mathbb R$ is the set of real numbers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Q^+\\subset  \\mathbb R^+ \\subset \\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nHowever, note that, for example, $\\mathbb Q \\not\\subset  \\mathbb R^+,$ since $0\\in\\mathbb Q$ yet $0\\notin \\mathbb R^+.$\n\nFrom these basic inclusions, we can now deduce some subset relationships for other special numerical sets. For example, recall that [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] represents the set of all non-negative integers. \n\n* Obviously, [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] is a proper subset of [math]\\mathbb{Z}[/math] since all the elements of [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] are integers but there are some integers, say, [math]-1,[/math] that does not belong to [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.[/math] So, [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+ \\subset \\mathbb{Z}.[/math]\n\n* On the other hand, [math]\\mathbb{N}[/math] is a proper subset of [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] since all natural number lie in [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] but [math]0 \\notin \\mathbb{N}.[/math] So, [math]\\mathbb{N} \\subset \\mathbb{Z}_0^+.[/math]\n\nTherefore, we have [math]\\mathbb{N} \\subset \\mathbb{Z}_0^+ \\subset \\mathbb{Z}.[/math]" :tutorial/id #uuid "c336106d-52a5-5d56-a98f-c64cd26eef4b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8317 :tutorial/title "Subsets of Special Sets"} :step/id #uuid "5386697a-6e4f-5619-83d7-53f1064c31f2" :step/math-academy-id 152767 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055559}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055559 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054631 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054444 :question/id #uuid "f2840f6f-da8b-519d-a191-f19d37fa7957" :question/math-academy-id "e-13197"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049887}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399131}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"} :step/id #uuid "053e7255-8298-5305-9c62-d263e5b3b5ba" :step/math-academy-id 152768 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055560}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056073 :tutorial/content "The notation \n\n$$\nA \\supseteq B\n$$\n\nreads \"$A$ is a **superset** of $B$\", and is equivalent to $B \\subseteq A.$ This is similar to how we can write either $x \\leq y$ or $y \\geq x$. The two notations mean the same thing. \n\nSimilarly, $A \\supset B$ reads \"$A$ is a **proper superset** of $B$,\" and is equivalent to $B \\subset A.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d87be2be-dac5-52e2-bcbd-7c0ad0d17032" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9065 :tutorial/title "Superset Notation"} :step/id #uuid "d545e0c5-338a-5d32-8901-ef36ab148f6e" :step/math-academy-id 152771 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055561}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054632 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054445 :question/id #uuid "e8db159d-7a1b-52ab-be96-6b1fd3eabb29" :question/math-academy-id "e-13198"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351173}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"} :step/id #uuid "7b56c42d-9c31-515e-ae69-cd2b8675b68c" :step/math-academy-id 152772 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055596}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056075 :tutorial/content "You might wonder why we need to define a universal set. Can't we just let the universal set be the set of everything?\n\nThe answer is no. If we don't specify a well-defined universal set, we encounter a nonsensical situation called **Russell's paradox.**\n\nFirst, suppose there exists a set $\\Omega$ that contains absolutely everything, including every mathematical object, every thought, every book, every person, animal, cell, and star in the universe, past, present, or future. \n\nRussell's paradox originates from the following idea:\n\n> *Define $S$ as the set that contains all sets that are not elements of themselves.*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> S = \\{A\\in \\Omega\\,:\\, A\\notin A\\}\n> $$\n\nMost sets you can think of are elements of $S.$ For example, the set $\\{ 1,2,3 \\}$ is in $S$ because $\\{ 1,2,3 \\}\\notin \\{ 1,2,3 \\}.$ \n\nIt takes a bit of thought to find sets that *aren't* members of $S.$ Such sets do exist, although typical examples are somewhat abstract: \n\n- The set containing all possible sets.\n\n- The set containing all possible thoughts.\n\n- The set $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX = \\Big\\{\\underbrace{\\big\\{ \\left\\{\\{\\ldots \\}\\right\\}\\big\\}}_{X}\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nHowever, what about $S$ itself? Is $S$ an element of $S?$ There are only two possibilities:\n\n- If $S\\in S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\notin S.$ But this is a contradiction because we cannot have $S\\in S$ *and* $S\\notin S.$\n\n- If $S\\notin S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we must have that $S\\in S.$ But this leads to the same contradiction as before!\n\nThe only way to resolve this is to conclude that $S\\notin \\Omega,$ but this is also a contradiction since $\\Omega$ contains absolutely everything!  So, there is no consistent answer to the question of whether $S$ itself is an element of $S.$ This is Russell's paradox, and when it was discovered, it shook the foundations of mathematics to its very core.\n\nThe issue, unknown to mathematicians until later, is that the object [math]S[/math] is not a set. If it were, Russell's paradox would not occur since we should always be able to determine whether an object is a member of a set or not.\n\nLater, it was realized that if a set is defined using a predicate, we should ensure that all sets involved are subsets of some larger set $U$ (i.e., a universal set) where we can always determine which elements are members of $U$ and which are not, at least in principle.\n\nTo see how this resolves Russell's paradox, suppose $U$ is a universal set. Let's refine our definition of $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\{A\\subseteq U\\,:\\,A\\notin A\\}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume $S$ is a set, so $S\\subseteq U$ for some well-defined $U.$ Once again, we'll ask the question of whether $S\\in S{:}$\n\n- If $S\\in S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\notin S.$ But this is a contradiction.\n\n- If $S\\notin S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\in S.$ But this is also a contradiction.\n\nThus, to resolve the paradox, we're left with no choice but to conclude that $S\\not\\subseteq U.$ This indicates that something is fundamentally wrong with our definition of $S,$ and no such set can exist because if it were a set, it would belong to a universal set. \n\n* If [math]S\\notin S,[/math] then by the definition of [math]S[/math] and applying De Morgan's law for conjunctions, we have\n\\begin{align*}\nS\\notin S \\Leftrightarrow \\lnot (S\\subseteq U\\land  S\\notin S)  \\equiv (S\\not\\subseteq U) \\lor (S\\in S).\n\\end{align*}" :tutorial/id #uuid "0e2d16c6-3382-5ba2-8d4b-949482eb4f07" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9388 :tutorial/title "Russell's Paradox: Why We Need a Universal Set"} :step/id #uuid "7280da1b-4410-550f-9a00-d46456526bfc" :step/math-academy-id 164820}] :activity/title "Subsets" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054909}]}
